---
import Doc from '@/layouts/Doc.astro'
import SearchDemo from '@/components/SearchDemo.astro'
import CodeSwitcher from '@/components/CodeSwitcher.astro'
import Command from '@/components/Command.astro'

/* These samples are written here rather than read from an example program,
   because they are queries a reader runs against their own daemon, not code
   that CI can execute. Every parameter in them is one the server parses. */
const finding = [
  {
    lang: 'curl' as const,
    label: 'cURL',
    shiki: 'bash',
    code: `# Failed runs from the last day that mention both words, anywhere in a
# span name, a prompt or a completion.
curl -s --get http://localhost:4317/v1/api/traces \\
  --data-urlencode 'q=Leeds depot' \\
  --data-urlencode 'status=error' \\
  --data-urlencode 'from=-24h'

# Every step that called one model and failed, across every run at once.
curl -s --get http://localhost:4317/v1/api/spans \\
  --data-urlencode 'model=gpt-4o-mini' \\
  --data-urlencode 'status=error' \\
  --data-urlencode 'min_duration_ms=2000'
`,
  },
  {
    lang: 'typescript' as const,
    label: 'TypeScript',
    shiki: 'typescript',
    code: `import { Mcpd } from '@mcpd/sdk'

const mcpd = new Mcpd()

const bad = await mcpd.listTraces({
  q: 'Leeds depot',
  status: 'error',
  from: '-24h',
  limit: 20,
})

for (const trace of bad.traces) {
  console.log(trace.traceId, trace.name, trace.status)
}`,
  },
  {
    lang: 'python' as const,
    label: 'Python',
    shiki: 'python',
    code: `from mcpd import Mcpd

mcpd = Mcpd()

bad = mcpd.list_traces(q="Leeds depot", status="error", from_="-24h", limit=20)

for trace in bad.traces:
    print(trace.trace_id, trace.name, trace.status.value)`,
  },
]

const discovery = [
  {
    lang: 'curl' as const,
    label: 'cURL',
    shiki: 'bash',
    code: `# Every environment this project has reported, for a filter box.
curl -s --get http://localhost:4317/v1/api/discovery \\
  --data-urlencode 'project=default' \\
  --data-urlencode 'facet=environment'

# Typeahead: the service names starting with "check".
curl -s --get http://localhost:4317/v1/api/discovery \\
  --data-urlencode 'facet=service_name' \\
  --data-urlencode 'prefix=check'

# The values one metric label takes.
curl -s --get http://localhost:4317/v1/api/discovery \\
  --data-urlencode 'facet=metric_label_value' \\
  --data-urlencode 'key=model'
`,
  },
  {
    lang: 'curl' as const,
    label: '200 OK',
    shiki: 'json',
    code: `{
  "facet": "environment",
  "values": [
    { "value": "production", "last_seen": "2026-09-03T08:04:18Z" },
    { "value": "staging", "last_seen": "2026-09-02T17:41:02Z" }
  ]
}`,
  },
  {
    lang: 'curl' as const,
    label: '501 Not Implemented',
    shiki: 'json',
    code: `{
  "error": "not_implemented",
  "capability": "discovery",
  "message": "this deployment stores signals in <backend>, which does not support discovery: ... GET /v1/api/capabilities lists what it does support."
}`,
  },
]

const badParam = [
  {
    lang: 'curl' as const,
    label: '400 Bad Request',
    shiki: 'json',
    code: `{
  "error": "bad_parameter",
  "field": "from",
  "message": "\\"yesterday\\" is not an RFC 3339 timestamp or a relative duration like -1h"
}`,
  },
]

const paging = [
  {
    lang: 'curl' as const,
    label: 'cURL',
    shiki: 'bash',
    code: `# The first page hands back a cursor in page.next_cursor.
curl -s --get http://localhost:4317/v1/api/traces \\
  --data-urlencode 'q=refund' \\
  --data-urlencode 'sort=cost' \\
  --data-urlencode 'limit=100'

# The next page: the same filters, the same sort, plus the cursor.
curl -s --get http://localhost:4317/v1/api/traces \\
  --data-urlencode 'q=refund' \\
  --data-urlencode 'sort=cost' \\
  --data-urlencode 'limit=100' \\
  --data-urlencode 'cursor=MzEzNzQyfDBhZjM...'
`,
  },
  {
    lang: 'typescript' as const,
    label: 'TypeScript',
    shiki: 'typescript',
    code: `let cursor: string | undefined

do {
  const page = await mcpd.listTraces({
    q: 'refund',
    sort: 'cost',
    order: 'desc',
    limit: 100,
    cursor,
  })

  for (const trace of page.traces) {
    console.log(trace.traceId, trace.cost?.totalCost ?? 'not priced')
  }

  // Loop on hasMore. There is no total to count towards.
  cursor = page.page.hasMore ? page.page.nextCursor : undefined
} while (cursor)`,
  },
  {
    lang: 'python' as const,
    label: 'Python',
    shiki: 'python',
    code: `cursor = None

while True:
    page = mcpd.list_traces(
        q="refund", sort="cost", order="desc", limit=100, cursor=cursor
    )

    for trace in page.traces:
        cost = trace.cost.total_cost if trace.cost else "not priced"
        print(trace.trace_id, cost)

    # Loop on has_more. There is no total to count towards.
    if not page.page.has_more:
        break
    cursor = page.page.next_cursor`,
  },
]
---

<Doc
  title="Finding a run"
  description="Full-text search over prompts and completions, every filter mcpd accepts on traces and spans, and the query that finds the answers a scorer marked down."
  lede="An investigation starts with a complaint, not a dashboard. This page is how you turn a complaint into the one run that caused it."
  og="docs"
>
  <h2>Search the payload, not the label</h2>
  <p>
    Most tracing tools index names and attributes. mcpd indexes the prompt and
    the completion as well, because what a person complains about is something
    the model said, and the words they quote back to you are the only handle you
    have. The <code>q</code> parameter is that index.
  </p>

  <SearchDemo class="not-prose my-8" />

  <h2>What q covers, exactly</h2>
  <p>
    The index holds three fields per span: its name, its input and its output.
    Input and output are JSON, so before indexing mcpd walks the payload and
    keeps only its strings and numbers, discarding the braces, the quotes and
    the keys. Indexing the raw JSON would make every punctuation mark a token
    and bury the words you are actually looking for.
  </p>
  <p>Six consequences are worth knowing before you rely on it:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A run matches when <strong>any single span in it</strong> matches. You get
      whole runs back; open one to see which step it was.
    </li>
    <li>
      Words are combined with AND. <code>q=refund delayed</code> finds spans
      containing both, anywhere in the indexed text, not next to each other.
    </li>
    <li>
      Matching is whole-word and case-insensitive, and diacritics are folded, so
      <code>resume</code> finds <code>résumé</code>. There is no stemming:
      <code>refund</code> does not find <code>refunds</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      The first 64 KiB of each of input and output goes into the index. Longer
      payloads are stored and displayed whole; only the tail of a very large
      prompt is unsearchable.
    </li>
    <li>
      Redaction runs before storage, so anything the redactor removed was never
      indexed either. See
      <a href="/docs/privacy" class="link">Redaction and retention</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      A span written more than once, as a long-running span is when it ends,
      replaces its index row, so search always reflects the current version.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>A stray quote is a search, not an error</h2>
  <p>
    The index underneath has its own query language: a double quote opens a
    phrase, and <code>NEAR</code>, <code>OR</code> and <code>*</code> are
    operators. Handing it your text unchanged would mean a customer message
    containing a quotation mark comes back as a syntax error instead of a
    result, and a search box that can throw is a search box people stop using.
  </p>
  <p>
    So your text never reaches that parser. mcpd splits what you typed on
    whitespace, strips any double quote out of each word, wraps each word as a
    literal term and joins them with AND. <code>q=say "no"</code> searches for
    the words <em>say</em> and <em>no</em>. <code>q=NEAR</code> searches for the
    word NEAR. Nothing you can type is a syntax error, and no operators are
    available to you. That is the trade, made in that direction on purpose.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <code>q</code> of nothing but whitespace is dropped rather than matching
    nothing, and an empty <code>q=</code> is treated as absent. Every parameter
    behaves that way, so a form that submits its empty filter boxes does not
    accidentally filter on the empty string.
  </p>

  <h2>Narrowing a run list</h2>
  <p>
    <code>GET /v1/api/traces</code> takes the following. Anything you leave out
    is not applied.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Parameter</th><th>What it selects</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>q</code></td>
        <td>Full text over each span's name, input and output.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>status</code></td>
        <td>
          <code>unset</code>, <code>ok</code>, <code>error</code> or
          <code>cancelled</code>. A run is <code>error</code> when any span in it
          failed, even one whose error was caught.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>kind</code></td>
        <td>
          The kind of the run's root span, such as <code>agent_run</code> or
          <code>workflow_run</code>.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>name</code></td>
        <td>The root span's name, exactly. Not a prefix and not a pattern.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>tags</code></td>
        <td>
          Comma separated. A run must carry <em>every</em> tag listed, not any
          of them.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr><td><code>session_id</code></td><td>Every run in one conversation.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>thread_id</code></td><td>Every run in one thread.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>user_id</code></td><td>Every run for one end user.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>environment</code></td><td>One environment, such as <code>production</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>model</code></td>
        <td>Runs with at least one span against this model id.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>from</code>, <code>to</code></td>
        <td>The window, measured against the run's start time.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>min_duration_ms</code></td>
        <td>Runs at least this slow, in milliseconds.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>min_cost</code></td>
        <td>
          Runs that cost at least this. A run with no priced spans counts as
          zero, so any positive value excludes it.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>has_score</code></td>
        <td>Runs carrying a score from this scorer id.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>score_below</code></td>
        <td>With <code>has_score</code>: runs that scorer put under this value.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>sort</code></td>
        <td>
          <code>start_time</code>, <code>duration</code>, <code>cost</code>,
          <code>span_count</code> or <code>score</code>. Defaults to
          <code>start_time</code>. <code>score</code> orders runs by the lowest
          score any scorer gave them, so ascending puts the worst run first; a
          run with no score ranks as zero.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>order</code></td>
        <td><code>asc</code> or <code>desc</code>. Defaults to <code>desc</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>limit</code></td>
        <td>Page size, clamped to 1 to 500. Defaults to 50.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>cursor</code></td>
        <td>The opaque cursor from the previous page.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>project</code></td>
        <td>
          Which project to read. Defaults to the daemon's own default project,
          so a single-project install never has to pass it.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    <code>name</code>, the correlation ids and <code>environment</code> are
    equality tests, which is what makes them cheap. If you want a partial match
    on a name, use <code>q</code>: span names are in the text index too.
  </p>

  <h2>What a list row costs</h2>
  <p>
    There is no second, lighter run list, because the first one is already
    light. The stored trace projection carries no input, no output and no
    attributes at all: names, counts, timings, cost, tags and correlation, and
    nothing a model said. Opening a run is what fetches the payload.
  </p>
  <p>
    Measured on one realistic run, an eight-step support agent that retrieves
    three policy documents, calls two tools and makes two model calls with real
    prompts and completions: the list row is <strong>577 bytes</strong> and the
    whole run is <strong>10,717 bytes</strong>, a <strong>94.6% reduction</strong>.
    That is this daemon, on that run, over the real HTTP surface. Your runs will
    differ, and the direction is what matters: the saving grows with the size of
    the prompts, because the part that grows is the part the list does not
    carry.
  </p>
  <p>
    The figure is produced by a test rather than typed here, so it cannot go
    stale in one place without failing in the other. Run
    <code>cargo test -p mcpd-server --test payload_size -- --nocapture</code>
    and it prints both byte counts. A field added to the projection that carries
    payload fails that test.
  </p>

  <h2>Anything else is refused, by name</h2>
  <p>
    That table is the whole vocabulary. A parameter that is not in it is refused
    with a 400 naming it, rather than accepted and dropped, so a typo such as
    <code>?statuss=error</code> is caught at once instead of returning every run
    in the project. A plausible wrong answer is worse than an error, because
    there is nothing to notice.
  </p>
  <p>
    A client that sends a filter only a newer daemon understands gets a 400 from
    an older one, naming the parameter, rather than a wider result set.
  </p>
  <p>
    Some refusals say more than "unknown". mcpd records
    <code>service_name</code>, <code>service_version</code>,
    <code>resource_id</code>, <code>run_id</code>, <code>request_id</code>,
    <code>experiment_id</code> and <code>release</code> on every trace, and you
    read them on the run itself rather than filter the run list by them. Of the
    seven, only
    <code>service_name</code> is a
    <a href="#filling-a-filter-box" class="link">discovery</a> facet, so
    discovery lists its values and none of the others'. Those seven are refused
    with a message that says so, because <code>service_name=checkout-api</code>
    is not a typo and telling somebody it is an unknown parameter sends them
    hunting a spelling mistake that is not there.
  </p>

  <p>
    The rule is not the run list's alone. <code>GET /v1/api/spans</code>,
    <code>/v1/api/logs</code>, <code>/v1/api/scores</code>,
    <code>/v1/api/metrics</code> and <code>/v1/api/feedback</code> each enforce
    their own vocabulary the same way. The score list takes no
    <code>order</code>, because it is newest first and its cursor compares one
    way only. The feedback list reads a whole project, newest first, and
    narrows by <code>trace_id</code>, <code>span_id</code>, <code>kind</code>,
    <code>author_kind</code> and a <code>from</code> and <code>to</code> window,
    with a cursor for the next page. The log list filters on a line's
    correlation: <code>environment</code>, <code>service_name</code>,
    <code>service_version</code>, <code>session_id</code>,
    <code>thread_id</code>, <code>user_id</code>, <code>run_id</code>,
    <code>request_id</code>, <code>experiment_id</code> and
    <code>release</code>, each an exact match. Send a user id as you know it;
    it is hashed the way the stored one was.
  </p>

  <h2 id="filling-a-filter-box">Filling a filter box</h2>
  <p>
    A filter is only usable if you know what to type into it, and the values are
    not a fixed list: they are whatever the services reporting to a project
    happen to send. <code>GET /v1/api/discovery</code> answers that. Ask for one
    dimension at a time with <code>facet</code>, and it returns the values that
    dimension has taken in the project, ordered by value, each with when a
    signal last carried it.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Facet</th><th>What it lists</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>entity_kind</code></td><td>Agent, workflow, tool, model, scorer, processor, pipeline or service.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>entity_name</code></td><td>The names those things have, such as the tools that were called.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>service_name</code></td><td>The services that have reported.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>environment</code></td><td>The environments seen, such as <code>production</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>tag</code></td><td>Every tag any run carries.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>metric_name</code></td><td>The metric names recorded, including the ones mcpd derives.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>metric_label_key</code></td><td>Every label key seen in the project.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>metric_label_value</code></td><td>The values of one label. Needs <code>key</code>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    <code>prefix</code> narrows the list for typeahead, and it matches literally
    and case sensitively, because these values are what a filter will be
    compared against and folding here would offer one that then matches nothing.
    <code>limit</code> caps the answer at 1000. Values are ordered by value and
    not by recency, so a list somebody is reading does not reshuffle under them
    between polls.
  </p>
  <p>
    Know one detail before you build against it.
    <code>metric_label_key</code> lists every label key seen anywhere in the
    project, not the keys of one named metric: the distinct-value list is keyed
    by the label, not by the metric that carried it.
  </p>

  <h2>Refreshing a list without running it again</h2>
  <p>
    <code>GET /v1/api/deltas</code> returns what has arrived since a cursor,
    oldest first, for <code>traces</code>, <code>spans</code> or
    <code>logs</code>. Hold the cursor from the last poll, send it back, and
    apply the rows to the list you already hold.
  </p>
  <p>
    A poll is not a page, and the two cursors are deliberately not
    interchangeable. Paging walks a fixed result set backwards in the sort you
    chose; polling walks forwards in arrival order and never ends. The watermark
    is a row's arrival time and its id together, because a batch writes every row
    in it with one timestamp and a watermark that could only say "after this
    nanosecond" would either replay the whole batch or skip the rest of it. An
    empty poll returns the cursor unchanged: moving it forward would drop a row
    that lands a moment later with an earlier arrival time.
  </p>
  <p>
    Polling covers traces, spans and logs. Scores and feedback are stamped with
    the timestamp the caller sent and carry no server-set arrival time, so a
    watermark over them would either skip a late row or repeat every row for
    ever. <code>signal=scores</code> is refused rather than answered with an
    empty page.
  </p>

  <h3>A live tail</h3>
  <p>
    Send the same request with <code>Accept: text/event-stream</code> and it
    stays open. The daemon polls every second and sends each batch that
    brings rows as one <code>delta</code> event holding the response above,
    with the cursor after it as the event id. A client that drops reconnects
    with that cursor and misses nothing. A tail closes after thirty minutes,
    and the client reconnects the same way.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="follow new runs as they arrive"
    command="curl -N -H 'accept: text/event-stream' 'localhost:4317/v1/api/deltas?signal=traces'"
  />

  <h2>What this deployment can do</h2>
  <p>
    mcpd has one storage trait and three backends behind it, and they differ in
    places. On ClickHouse a delete is accepted and applied after the call
    returns, so the counts in its answer say what matched rather than what has
    left the disk. On SQLite and Postgres the rows are gone when the call
    returns.
    <code>GET /v1/api/capabilities</code> is where that is written down: which
    backend is behind the daemon, what <code>q</code> matches, whether case is
    folded outside ASCII, whether a delete is on disk when the call returns, and
    whether discovery and delta polling answer at all.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read it once at connect time and offer only what the deployment answers. An
    endpoint whose capability is false refuses with <code>501</code> and a body
    whose <code>capability</code> field names the same word the capability set
    publishes, so the two can never disagree and a client that met the refusal
    can look it up rather than retrying something that will never change.
  </p>
  <p>
    SQLite, Postgres and ClickHouse all answer both discovery and delta polling,
    so the 501 below shows the shape of a refusal rather than a response you
    will see from them. Read the capability set all the same: the three differ
    on whether a delete is on disk when the call returns, and on how retention
    is enforced. All three fold case outside ASCII.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" samples={discovery} title="what can I filter on?" />

  <h2>Windows</h2>
  <p>
    <code>from</code> and <code>to</code> accept an RFC 3339 timestamp, or a
    relative shorthand: a minus sign, a number and one of <code>s</code>,
    <code>m</code>, <code>h</code> or <code>d</code>. <code>-15m</code>,
    <code>-1h</code> and <code>-7d</code> all work. The shorthand exists because
    typing an ISO timestamp by hand to look at the last hour is friction nobody
    should pay.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>from</code> is inclusive and <code>to</code> is exclusive, and both
    compare against the time the run <em>started</em>. A long run that began
    before your window and was still going inside it is not returned. If you are
    hunting something that was running at 09:00, widen <code>from</code>
    backwards rather than narrowing it.
  </p>
  <p>
    A value that is neither a timestamp nor a duration is refused by name rather
    than ignored. Every parameter on every list endpoint fails this way, because
    a filter that is silently dropped returns a plausible wrong answer, and a
    plausible wrong answer is worse than an error.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" samples={badParam} title="from=yesterday" />

  <h2>The query that finds bad answers</h2>
  <p>
    <code>has_score=answer_relevance&amp;score_below=0.5</code> returns every run
    that scorer put under half. This is the one to reach for when the complaint
    is about quality rather than a failure, because a bad answer is usually a run
    that succeeded.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>has_score</code> on its own returns every run that scorer has looked
    at. <code>score_below</code> on its own does nothing at all: a threshold with
    no scorer attached has no meaning, and mcpd would have to guess which scorer
    you meant. It does not guess.
    <a href="/docs/evaluation" class="link">Scores and feedback</a> covers where
    those values come from.
  </p>

  <h2>Searching steps instead of runs</h2>
  <p>
    <code>GET /v1/api/spans</code> searches individual steps across every run at
    once. Use it for questions about a step rather than a session: which tool
    call is slow, which calls to one model errored, how long a retrieval takes.
  </p>
  <p>
    It takes <code>q</code>, <code>kind</code>, <code>status</code>,
    <code>name</code>, <code>model</code>, <code>trace_id</code>,
    <code>from</code>, <code>to</code>, <code>min_duration_ms</code>,
    <code>sort</code>, <code>order</code>, <code>limit</code> and
    <code>cursor</code>, with the same meanings and the same defaults. There is
    no <code>tags</code>, <code>session_id</code> or <code>user_id</code> filter
    here, because those belong to the run, and all three are refused by name
    rather than accepted and dropped.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>sort</code> takes fewer values here than on the run list:
    <code>start_time</code>, <code>duration</code> or <code>cost</code>, and
    nothing else. A span carries no span count and no score rollup, so
    <code>span_count</code> and <code>score</code> have nothing to sort on and
    are refused by name rather than ignored.
  </p>
  <p>
    On a run, <code>model</code> asks whether any span used it. On a span,
    <code>model</code> is that span's own model. The same word, two questions.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" samples={finding} title="find it" />

  <h2>Paging, and why the cursor is opaque</h2>
  <p>
    A list returns at most <code>limit</code> rows and a
    <code>page</code> object. <code>page.next_cursor</code> is present when there
    is more, and <code>page.has_more</code> says the same thing as a boolean.
  </p>
  <p>
    Paging is keyset, not offset. The cursor names the last row you were given,
    and the next page starts strictly after it. Offset paging would drift:
    traces arrive continuously, so by the time you asked for rows 50 to 99 the
    window has shifted under you and you read one run twice while another never
    appears at all. In an investigation, the run you miss is the one you were
    looking for.
  </p>
  <p>
    The cursor is a hex-encoded pair of the sort value and the row id, and you
    should treat it as meaningless. It is opaque so that the encoding can change
    without breaking every caller, and because the value inside it is a different
    quantity for each sort: nanoseconds for <code>start_time</code> and
    <code>duration</code>, a scaled integer for <code>cost</code>, a plain count
    for <code>span_count</code>, the run's lowest score for <code>score</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    That has one rule attached. <strong>Send a cursor back with the same filters,
    the same <code>sort</code> and the same <code>order</code> that produced
    it.</strong> mcpd does not check that you did, and a cursor replayed against a
    different sort compares against the wrong column and skips rows. A cursor
    that has been edited or truncated is refused with
    <code>invalid_query</code> and the message <code>malformed cursor</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Loop on <code>has_more</code> rather than counting towards a number.
    <code>page.total</code> is reserved for a backend that can count matches
    cheaply, and the built-in SQLite store leaves it empty.
  </p>

  <CodeSwitcher class="not-prose my-6" samples={paging} title="page through it" />

  <h2>Once you have the run</h2>
  <p>
    A trace id from any of these queries goes to
    <code>GET /v1/api/traces/{'{trace_id}'}</code>, which returns the run and every
    span in it, already ordered so a waterfall renders with no second request.
    Add <code>include=logs,scores,feedback</code> to get everything attached to
    it in the same response.
  </p>
  <p>
    From there,
    <a href="/docs/cost" class="link">Cost accounting</a> explains the figures on
    each step, and the
    <a href="/docs/reference/api" class="link">HTTP API reference</a> lists every
    endpoint and field as the specification declares them.
  </p>
</Doc>
